# Apache configuration for the prerendered static build.
#
# Lives in client/public/ so vite copies it into dist/public on every build.
# It was previously added by hand to the deploy branch only, which meant any
# rebuild of that branch silently dropped it.
#
# No SPA rewrite here on purpose. scripts/prerender.mjs writes every one of the
# application's routes to <route>/index.html, so Apache's ordinary file and
# directory resolution already serves them. Rewriting unmatched paths to
# /index.html — which this file used to do — answers every unknown URL with
# HTTP 200 and the homepage, a soft 404 that search engines index as a real
# page. ErrorDocument returns the prerendered 404 page with a genuine 404
# status instead, matching the express handler in server/_core/vite.ts that the
# e2e suite exercises.

Options -Indexes

ErrorDocument 404 /404.html

# Do not add <Limit> or <LimitExcept> here.
#
# This file previously carried:
#
#     <Files "apply.php">
#         <LimitExcept POST>
#             Require all denied
#         </LimitExcept>
#     </Files>
#
# to stop a GET returning the endpoint's source on a host without PHP. On
# Apache 2.4 that behaved exactly as intended — GET 403, POST executed — and it
# was tested there. This host runs LiteSpeed, which denied *every* method
# including POST, so /api/apply.php answered 403 to the form itself and every
# application fell back to the mail draft.
#
# Measured against the live site, which is what settled it: GET and POST both
# 403, while /api/nonexistent.php returned 404 and POST to a static file
# returned 200 — so the denial was scoped to this rule rather than a
# server-wide policy.
#
# The method restriction was never load-bearing. apply.php answers a non-POST
# with 405 itself, and it holds no credential, because sending goes through the
# local MTA rather than authenticated SMTP. Protecting the source of an
# open-source file was not worth a directive whose meaning changes between the
# server it was tested on and the server it runs on.
